%[text] # Calculate a Closed-Box Loudspeaker
%[text] This example calculates a loudspeaker system with a B&C 21SW152-8 woofer in a closed box: the electrical impedance, the sound pressure level and the effect of the volume of the box. See the class pages of [`comp.ClosedBox`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','closedbox','closedboxdoc.m'))) and [`lspsys`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','lspsys','lspsysdoc.m'))) for the model.
%%
%[text] ## Create the driver
%[text] Create a `comp.Driver` with the Thiele-Small parameters from the datasheet of the B&C 21SW152-8.
driver = comp.Driver;
driver.Re = 6;
driver.Le = 2.2e-3;
driver.Qes = 0.38;
driver.Qms = 6.4;
driver.Fs = 32;
driver.Sd = 1680e-4;
driver.Vas = 200e-3;
%%
%[text] ## Mount the driver in a closed box
%[text] Mount the driver in a closed box with a volume of 200 L, equal to the $ V\_{as} $ of the driver.
box = comp.ClosedBox;
box.Driver = driver;
box.RearVolume = 200e-3;
%%
%[text] ## Solve the system
%[text] Put the closed box in the `lspsys` object `loudspeaker` and calculate the result `res` for the default frequencies from 20 Hz to 20 kHz and the default source voltage of 2.83 V.
loudspeaker = lspsys;
loudspeaker.Enclosure = box;
res = loudspeaker.createResult;
%%
%[text] ## Electrical impedance
%[text] The peak of the impedance lies at the resonance frequency of the driver in the box, above the free-air resonance $ f\_s $ of 32 Hz because the air in the box stiffens the suspension.
semilogx(res.Frequency,abs(res.ElectricalImpedance),LineWidth=1.5) %[output:30666df4]
grid on %[output:30666df4]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:30666df4]
ylabel("Electrical impedance [\Omega]") %[output:30666df4]
title("Electrical impedance") %[output:30666df4]
%%
%[text] ## Sound pressure level
%[text] The sound pressure level at a distance of `MicRadius`, 1 m by default, for radiation into a half space.
semilogx(res.Frequency,res.SoundPressureLevel,LineWidth=1.5) %[output:9b517470]
grid on %[output:9b517470]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:9b517470]
ylabel("Sound pressure level [dB]") %[output:9b517470]
title("Sound pressure level at 1 m") %[output:9b517470]
%%
%[text] ## Effect of the volume of the box
%[text] A smaller box stiffens the suspension more: the low-frequency roll-off moves up and the response around the resonance rises.
volumes = [50 100 200 400]*1e-3;
levels = zeros(numel(volumes),numel(loudspeaker.Frequency));
for k = 1:numel(volumes)
    box.RearVolume = volumes(k);
    loudspeaker.Enclosure = box;
    levels(k,:) = loudspeaker.createResult.SoundPressureLevel;
end
semilogx(loudspeaker.Frequency,levels,LineWidth=1.5) %[output:16cf7017]
grid on %[output:16cf7017]
xlim([20 1000]) %[output:16cf7017]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:16cf7017]
ylabel("Sound pressure level [dB]") %[output:16cf7017]
legend(compose("%g L",volumes*1e3),Location="southeast") %[output:16cf7017]
title("Sound pressure level for different box volumes") %[output:16cf7017]

